\label{def:weighted-adj}
Given a symmetric real-valued function \(f(x,y)\) and a real-valued function \(g(x)\), the \emph{weighted adjacency-degree matrix} of \(G\) is defined by
\[
A_{fg}(G):=
\begin{cases}
g(d_i), & i=j,\\[2pt]
f(d_i,d_j), & v_i v_j\in E(G),\\[2pt]
0, & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
\]

\label{def:Pfg}
The polynomial \(P_{fg,n}\) is defined recursively by
\[
\begin{cases}
P_{fg,0}=\lambda-g(1),\\[2pt]
P_{fg,1}=\bigl[\lambda-g(k)\bigr]\bigl[\lambda-g(1)\bigr]-f^{2}(1,k)(k-1),\\[2pt]
P_{fg,n}=\bigl[\lambda-g(k)\bigr]P_{fg,n-1}-f^{2}(k,k)(k-1)P_{fg,n-2},
\quad n\ge 2.
\end{cases}
\]

\label{cor:special-cases}
The characteristic polynomials of the matrices associated with \(D_{n,k}\) and \(B_{n,k}\) are listed in Tables~\ref{tab:Dnk} and~\ref{tab:Bnk}. In these tables, \(\beta=1-\alpha\) for the \(A_\alpha\) matrix.

